# ************************************
#   Comments + Keyword Clarification

#    Anton, Calculus, 0.1.9b
# ************************************
# DESCRIPTION
# Determine the domain and range of f(x) = Ax/|x|

# ENDDESCRIPTION
## DBsubject('Calculus')
## DBchapter('Before Calculus')
## DBsection('Functions')
## KEYWORDS('Functions', 'Domain', 'Range')
## TitleText1('Calculus')
## EditionText1('9')
## AuthorText1('Anton')
## Section1('0.1')
## Problem1('9b')
## Author('')
## Institution('John Wiley & Sons')

DOCUMENT();
loadMacros('PGstandard.pl', 'PGML.pl', 'PGcourse.pl');

Context("Numeric");
#************************************************
#  Create function of form A(x - B)/abs(x - B)
#************************************************
$A    = random(1, 9, 1);
$B    = random(1, 9, 1);
$Func = Formula("$A(x - $B)/|x - $B|")->reduce;
#**************
#   Answers
#**************
Context("Interval");
$Domain = Compute("(-inf,$B) U ($B,inf)");
$Range  = List(-$A, $A);

BEGIN_PGML
Find the domain and range of the function [``f(x) = [$Func] ``]. Express each in
interval notation.

a) Domain: [_]{$Domain}

b) Range: [_]{$Range}

[@ helpLink('interval') @]*

END_PGML

BEGIN_PGML_SOLUTION
For a rational function such as [``f(x) = [$Func] ``], the domain is restricted to
all values of [`x`] that do not produce a zero in the denominator.
  
For this function, the domain may include all real values of [`x`] except for 
[`x = [$B]`]. In interval notation, this is [`[$Domain]`].
  
To determine the range of this function, note that [``[$Func]``] can attain only
two values: [$A] when [`x > [$B]`] and -[$A] when [`x < [$B]`].
  
Therefore, for the function [``f(x) = [$Func]``], the range is
{[` [$Range] `]}.
END_PGML_SOLUTION

ENDDOCUMENT();
